from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long, render_note


def investable_portfolio() -> str:
    body = '''<p>One of the easiest ways to make an AI program look active is to build a use-case inventory. Workshops generate ideas quickly. Functions contribute opportunities. Teams classify them by value and feasibility. Soon there is a spreadsheet with dozens — sometimes hundreds — of possibilities.</p><p>The problem is that an inventory is not a portfolio.</p><p>A portfolio represents commitments under constraint. It shows what the organization is prepared to fund, what it expects to learn, what must be true before more money is released, and which ideas will not progress. That requires a different kind of conversation.</p><blockquote>If everything remains a priority, the use-case list has documented demand. It has not made an investment decision.</blockquote><h2>The first mistake is ranking ideas too early</h2><p>I have seen teams move directly from ideation to scoring. Each opportunity gets a number for business value, technical feasibility, strategic fit or risk. The output looks disciplined, but the precision can be misleading. Early estimates are often based on very different levels of evidence.</p><p>A mature business case should not be required for every early idea. But the organization should separate what it knows from what it assumes. That makes the next question much more useful: what is the cheapest piece of evidence that would materially change the investment decision?</p><p>For one idea that may be data availability. For another it may be whether users will change a workflow. For a third it may be whether the economics still work once integration, monitoring and operating costs are included.</p><h2>I prefer evidence stages to one permanent score</h2><p>A single portfolio score tends to follow an initiative for too long. My preference is to let the decision criteria change as the initiative matures.</p><p><strong>At discovery:</strong> Is the problem important enough to investigate, is there a credible owner, and is AI genuinely relevant?</p><p><strong>Before a pilot:</strong> What assumption are we testing, what evidence would count as success, and what is the minimum cost of learning?</p><p><strong>Before production:</strong> Is the value mechanism still credible, can the data and controls be owned, and is adoption designed into the workflow?</p><p><strong>Before scale:</strong> Do we have evidence that the result is repeatable, economically defensible and operable without exceptional support?</p><h2>The portfolio meeting should be allowed to say no</h2><p>If the only acceptable outcomes are “continue” or “wait,” weak initiatives accumulate. Teams protect sunk effort. Central AI functions become responsible for keeping too many experiments alive. Capacity gets spread across work that is individually plausible but collectively unfocused.</p><p>I want four legitimate decisions on the table: <strong>invest, test, hold, stop.</strong></p><p>“Test” means there is a specific uncertainty worth resolving. “Hold” means the idea may have merit but should not consume current capacity. “Stop” is not failure; it is evidence that the portfolio is actually making choices.</p><h2>The hardest variable is usually not technical feasibility</h2><p>Technical feasibility matters, but in enterprise settings I often find more uncertainty around ownership, process change and value capture. A solution can work and still fail to produce value because nobody changes the surrounding workflow, the benefit lands in a different budget, or the business owner is interested but not accountable.</p><p>That is why I do not want a portfolio conversation dominated by “Can we build it?” The more useful question is: “What would have to become true for this to deserve the next level of investment?”</p><div class="rule"><strong>My rule: an AI portfolio is a sequence of investment decisions, not a catalogue of possibilities.</strong><p>The quality of the portfolio becomes visible in what gets stopped, what gets tested cheaply and where the organization is willing to concentrate attention.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Portfolio discipline</span><strong>Four decisions should always be legitimate.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div><strong>Invest</strong> — evidence supports the next commitment.</div><div><strong>Test</strong> — resolve one material uncertainty cheaply.</div><div><strong>Hold</strong> — credible idea, wrong timing or capacity.</div><div><strong>Stop</strong> — evidence no longer supports attention.</div></div><p>The point is not a better scoring spreadsheet. It is a clearer investment conversation.</p>'''
    return render_long("investable_portfolio", body, aside)


def adoption_metric() -> str:
    return render_note(
        "adoption_metric",
        "Usage is a signal. Adoption is a change in the work.",
        "I am cautious when AI adoption is reported mainly through licenses, logins or monthly active users. Those numbers are useful for reach, but they can look healthy while the underlying workflow remains exactly the same. For me, stronger evidence is closer to the work: did a decision become faster or better, did a step disappear, did people stop using the old workaround, did cycle time or rework change, and does the behavior persist after the launch campaign? I still track usage. I just would not call it adoption on its own.",
    )
